Figure 8 and 9 Interpretation (Systems Engineering Summary):

To keep the link's goodput above 90 percent using standard 1000-bit packets, we must hold the bit error rate strictly below 
. However, changing the packet length fundamentally shifts this operating requirement, as shown by the secondary goodput curves in Figure 9. If we shrink the payload to 100 bits, the system becomes much more resilient, tolerating error rates almost ten times higher before dropping below the 90 percent threshold. Conversely, pushing the packet length up to 10,000 bits causes the goodput to completely collapse at even trivial error rates. Therefore, a systems engineer must balance payload size against the channel's physical error rate to prevent the link from getting stuck in an endless retransmission loop.

