Bonus B Conclusion: By incorporating the 40-bit header, the objective function correctly penalizes both extremes of packet design. If 
 is too small (e.g., 50 bits), the header overhead dominates and wastes the channel's bandwidth. If 
 is too large (e.g., 2000 bits), the packet is almost guaranteed to be corrupted by the 
 error rate, causing endless retransmissions. Setting the derivative to zero mathematically proves the optimal sweet spot sits at exactly 
 bits, yielding a maximum achievable goodput of approximately 0.879. Our numerical array scan and the Monte Carlo simulation directly confirm this theoretical maximum.

